<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Domodossola</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Domodossola is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the main centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/val-dossola">Val d'Ossola</a> near the Swiss border, on the road and railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simplon-pass">Simplon Pass</a>. From 10 September to October 1944 it was the capital of the Partisan Republic of Ossola, one of the free zones set up by the Italian Resistance. Its Sacro Monte Calvario is part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/domodossola.jpg" alt="Domodossola" width="320" height="142" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlocat87 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_Domodossola_da_Monte_Calvario.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Domodossola lies at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, in the Toce plain at the crossroads of the Bognanco, Divedro, Antigorio, Vigezzo and Isorno valleys. It stands at the confluence of the Bogna and Toce rivers, 272 metres above sea level, and is the main centre of the Ossola. Much of its municipal territory is mountainous, and the ski resort of Domobianca lies within it, on the slopes of the Moncucco.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been inhabited since prehistoric times. Known as Oscella, the town was the chief town of the Lepontii when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> conquered the region in 12 BCE, and a Roman road linking Mediolanum to Lake Maggiore passed through it. Afterwards it belonged to the Ostrogothic Kingdom, whose king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a> fortified the nearby Mattarella hill. In the sixth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> sacked and burned it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> later drove them out. It then became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>-count of Novara, who built a castle there. A collegiate church of the eleventh century gave it the name Domus Oxile, which gradually became its modern name.</p><p>In 1381, after Swiss raids and the conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelphs-and-ghibellines">Guelphs and Ghibellines</a>, the town swore fealty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a>, Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in exchange for military protection. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> it came briefly under the French, then for nearly two centuries under the Spaniards and in 1706 under the Austrians. The Treaty of Worms of 1743 gave it to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, and in 1818 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-i-of-sardinia">Victor Emmanuel I</a> made it the capital of the new Province of Ossola. The opening of the Simplon Tunnel in 1906 put it again on a major international route. Industry and hydroelectric plants brought growth, and the population almost doubled between 1881 and 1921.</p><p>On 10 September 1944 Italian Resistance forces liberated Domodossola, which became the capital of the Republic of Ossola, one of the largest partisan republics in German-occupied northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. German and Fascist forces crushed the republic on 23 October 1944, and many inhabitants fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> to escape reprisals. In 1945 the city flag was awarded the Gold Medal of Military Valor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Domodossola is a city and comune in the Province of Verbano-Cusio-Ossola in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It is the main railway hub in northern Piedmont and an international stopping point between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and Brig in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simplon-pass">Simplon Pass</a>. Its best-known landmark is the Sacro Monte Calvario, a pilgrimage site and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, on the same hill as the remains of the medieval castle of Mattarella. Its name is widely known in Italy from the spelling alphabet, in which D stands for Domodossola.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Domodossola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In September 1944 Italian partisan formations drove German and Fascist garrisons out of the Val d'Ossola, near the Swiss border, and entered <b>Domodossola</b> on 10 September.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-ossola">Republic of Ossola</a></li></ul></section>
